WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.969 --> 00:00:03.820
We have a lot of choices
to make about our diet.

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:05.940
Add to that, doing
the right thing

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.120
when it comes
to preventing

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.130
and treating a
chronic disease,

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:11.679
fighting a virus,
or losing weight,

00:00:11.679 --> 00:00:13.490
and suddenly our
nutrition choices

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:15.710
can seem almost
overwhelming.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:18.420
Well, I’m here to help.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.400
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.250
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:27.140
Today we conclude our two-part
series on fasting and cancer.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.560
And we start with a story

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.730
about how the benefits
of short term fasting

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:33.550
translate in the
clinical setting.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.250
The first randomized
prospective clinical evaluation

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:39.360
of the effects of fasting
on chemotherapy,

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.940
the incidence of
chemotherapy-induced nausea

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:46.370
and vomiting in cancer patients
was published in 2014,

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:49.370
but the patients were dogs.

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:52.570
Cancer-bearing dogs presenting
at a veterinary hospital were

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:56.620
randomized to be fasted for
24 hours before chemotherapy,

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:00.149
and those that were,
were significantly less likely

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:01.230
to suffer from vomiting

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:05.810
only 1 in 10 compared to 2
out of 3 in the non-fasted group,

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:11.270
which is great, but what can that
tell us about human medicine?

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.220
Evidently, not much.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.040
It is nearly impossible to
rely on most animal data

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.150
to predict whether or not
an intervention will have

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:22.880
a favorable clinical benefit-risk
ratio in human subjects.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.020
For example, mice have
a metabolic rate approximately

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.150
seven times higher
than humans,

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:32.729
so a single day of fasting can cut
lean body mass about 15%.

00:01:32.729 --> 00:01:37.031
That would take over a
month of fasting in people.

00:01:37.031 --> 00:01:40.659
So that dramatic study on mice
showing 100% alive versus

00:01:40.659 --> 00:01:43.850
100% dead on high dose chemo
depending on whether

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:48.299
they were fasted for 60 hours,
what can that really tell us?

00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:50.450
And when it comes to cancer,
rodents can bear

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.649
massive tumor loads,
whereas people generally

00:01:53.649 --> 00:01:56.400
waste away and die
when tumor masses

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.600
reach just a thousandth
of our body weight.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.600
You can't even necessarily
extrapolate from one rat to another

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.009
even within the same strain
bought from different dealers.

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:09.390
The only way to see
what happens in humans,

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:14.620
the only way to guarantee findings
are relevant is to study people.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:17.980
The theory is that combining
fasting cycles with chemotherapy

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:20.490
could extend the survival of
advanced-stage cancer patients

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:25.910
by both retarding tumor progression
and reducing side effects,

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:29.489
or even providing an alternative
to chemotherapy altogether

00:02:29.489 --> 00:02:31.190
in early-stage cancer patients,

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:37.400
but that's all contingent on it being
confirmed in human clinical trials.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.110
First there were case series:

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:41.989
several patients diagnosed
with a variety of cancers

00:02:41.989 --> 00:02:45.099
elected to undertake fasting
prior to chemotherapy

00:02:45.099 --> 00:02:47.140
and shared their experiences.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:50.550
Those who underwent chemo
both with and without fasting

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.780
reported a reduction of
fatigue, weakness, and

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.300
gastrointestinal side effects
while fasting; in fact felt better

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:01.300
across the board with zero
vomiting in the fasting group.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.470
The weight lost during the few days
of fasting was rapidly recovered

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:08.319
in most of the patients and did
not lead to any detectable harm,

00:03:08.319 --> 00:03:11.810
so overall was looking
feasible, safe,

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:14.629
with the potential to
ameliorate side effects.

00:03:14.629 --> 00:03:18.370
But only randomized clinical
studies could tell for sure,

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:21.019
and so here we go.

00:03:21.019 --> 00:03:24.209
Breast and ovarian cancer
patients fasting, starting 36 hours

00:03:24.209 --> 00:03:28.340
before and ending 24 hours
after chemotherapy,

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:32.030
and it did appear to improve
the quality of life and fatigue,

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:35.760
but another study found
no such beneficial effects.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:39.879
There did appear to perhaps
be less bone marrow toxicity

00:03:39.879 --> 00:03:43.620
given the higher counts of red blood
cells and platelet-making cells,

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:47.180
but no benefit when it came
to killing off white blood cells --

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:51.549
the immune system cells --
so that was a disappointment.

00:03:51.549 --> 00:03:53.329
Perhaps they didn't
fast long enough?

00:03:53.329 --> 00:03:56.900
They only fasted 24 hours
before and after.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:01.099
To find out the optimal duration,
20 cancer patients were split up

00:04:01.099 --> 00:04:06.890
into three groups, fasting for
24, 48, or a total of 72 hours.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:10.849
All those in the 24-hour group
suffered nausea after chemo,

00:04:10.849 --> 00:04:13.860
but less than half
in the 72-hour group.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.639
And most were vomiting
in the 24-hour group,

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:19.639
but none in the
longest fasting group.

00:04:19.639 --> 00:04:23.139
Longer fasting groups also tended
to suffer less nerve damage

00:04:23.139 --> 00:04:25.820
and less serious bone
marrow suppression.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:28.419
Even after 24 hours of fasting,
two cycles of chemo can

00:04:28.419 --> 00:04:32.700
knock people's white blood cells
down to sub-optimal levels, but

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:38.889
with 72 hours, chemo knocked their
immune system down but not out.

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:43.130
OK, so short-term fasting may reduce
chemotherapy-induced toxicity,

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:47.610
but what I want to know is
if it makes it work better?

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.919
A systematic review of 22 studies
found that overall fasting

00:04:50.919 --> 00:04:53.541
was found to not only reduce
chemotherapy side effects, like

00:04:53.541 --> 00:04:57.450
organ damage, immune suppression,
and chemotherapy-induced death,

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:00.320
but also to suppress tumor
progression, including

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:06.820
tumor growth and metastasis,
resulting in improved survival, but...

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:09.660
nearly all of the studies
were done on lab animals.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:13.110
The studies on humans are
limited to evaluating safety

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:14.460
and side effects.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:16.289
The tumor-
suppression effects of fasting --

00:05:16.289 --> 00:05:20.699
for example, its influence on tumor
growth, metastasis and prognosis --

00:05:20.699 --> 00:05:24.847
have not been evaluated until now.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.919
To be continued.

00:05:27.208 --> 00:05:29.532
In our final story we ask,

00:05:29.532 --> 00:05:35.193
how might we replicate the protective
effects of fasting with food?

00:05:35.541 --> 00:05:38.550
Short-term food withdrawal during
chemotherapy may begin to solve

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:41.850
the long-standing problem
with most cancer treatments:

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:46.470
how to kill the tumor
without killing the patient?

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:48.480
Short-term fasting --
for example,

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.430
for 48 hours before chemo
and 24 hours afterwards --

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:55.350
may reduce side effects, reduce
chemotherapy-induced toxicity.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:59.990
However, the potential tumor-
suppressing effects of fasting --

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:04.710
for example, its influence on tumor
growth, metastasis and prognosis --

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:07.910
have not been evaluated until now.

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:13.819
No, actually, sadly, they mean
"have not been evaluated until now,"

00:06:13.819 --> 00:06:17.669
meaning they have not
been evaluated yet, period.

00:06:17.669 --> 00:06:22.490
So in answering the question to fast
or not to fast before chemotherapy,

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:25.139
it should be emphasized
that the evidence provided

00:06:25.139 --> 00:06:28.990
by human studies
is still very limited.

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:31.270
Some argue that reducing
the side effects alone

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:33.050
could improve efficacy,

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:36.220
since patients could
withstand higher doses.

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:38.670
For example, the heart and
kidney damage associated

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:41.800
with the widely prescribed
anti-cancer drugs limit

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:44.449
their full therapeutic potential.

00:06:44.449 --> 00:06:46.080
It's not clear, though, that

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:49.710
maximizing the tolerated
chemo dose would achieve

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:52.479
longer survival or
better quality of life.

00:06:52.479 --> 00:06:55.360
For now, I think we should
just be satisfied with

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:59.800
the fewer side effects for
fewer side effects' sake.

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:03.720
Some cancer docs urge caution,
concerned about malnutrition.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.510
That concern is echoed
in official guidelines

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:08.680
on nutrition in cancer patients.

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:10.940
Because of the risks
of malnutrition and,

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:13.949
yeah, maybe a day
or two might not hurt,

00:07:13.949 --> 00:07:17.380
but because patients might
be tempted to prolong fasting

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:21.199
episodes without firm evidence
of a benefit, they feel

00:07:21.199 --> 00:07:26.599
fasting during chemotherapy
cannot be recommended.

00:07:26.599 --> 00:07:29.979
Fasting proponents agree that
patients with severe weight loss,

00:07:29.979 --> 00:07:33.319
muscle wasting or malnutrition --
probably not good candidates

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:35.550
for even short-term fasting.

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:38.039
But out of the hundreds of
patients that have been tracked,

00:07:38.039 --> 00:07:41.479
there's no evidence of severe adverse
events in terms of malnutrition

00:07:41.479 --> 00:07:46.110
or weight loss with short-term
fasting or fasting-mimicking diets.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:48.070
And those patients who did
experience a weight loss

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:50.729
during fasting typically
recovered their weight

00:07:50.729 --> 00:07:54.740
before the subsequent cycle
of chemo without detectable harm.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:08:00.789
But look, even if there is a risk,
we're talking about cancer here.

00:08:00.789 --> 00:08:04.159
Accepting trade-offs is kind
of the name of the game.

00:08:04.159 --> 00:08:06.949
People are willing to put up
with the risk of severe,

00:08:06.949 --> 00:08:10.379
even life-threatening side
effects all the time.

00:08:10.379 --> 00:08:13.009
And even if it just helped
alleviate some of the side effects,

00:08:13.009 --> 00:08:14.879
it might be worth a try.

00:08:14.879 --> 00:08:18.940
Of course, meanwhile Big Pharma
is busy trying to come up with drugs

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:21.820
aimed at reproducing the
beneficial effects of fasting.

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:25.360
Although fasting mimicking drugs
may be preferred by patients

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:28.629
who would rather pop a pill
than go without eating,

00:08:28.629 --> 00:08:32.610
it will be years before
they'll ever be on the market,

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:35.610
and it's hard to imagine drugs
will be developed that are

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:40.060
both as effective and
as safe as fasting.

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:42.510
So is there anything
we can tweak in our diet

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:46.410
to get similar benefits
without having to fast?

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:49.230
The way fasting works
is by reducing levels

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:52.420
of a cancer-promoting
growth hormone known as

00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:54.170
insulin-like growth factor 1.

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:57.339
It's the reduced levels
of IGF-1 that mediate

00:08:57.339 --> 00:09:00.920
the differential protection
of normal and cancer cells

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:02.380
in response to fasting

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:05.079
and improves chemo's
ability to kill cancer

00:09:05.079 --> 00:09:07.800
but spare normal cells.

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:09.149
How do we know this?

00:09:09.149 --> 00:09:13.930
Because restoration of IGF-1
was sufficient to reverse

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:14.992
the protective effect of fasting.

00:09:14.992 --> 00:09:15.992
Check it out.

00:09:15.992 --> 00:09:16.992
Here's the growth of two cancer cell
lines, breast cancer and melanoma,

00:09:16.992 --> 00:09:17.992
in a petri dish after being
exposed to chemotherapy.

00:09:17.992 --> 00:09:18.992
Survival rates dropped down
into the 60s or even 20s.

00:09:18.992 --> 00:09:19.992
But the same dose in starved
cells works even better.

00:09:19.992 --> 00:09:20.992
Same dose, same starvation,
but drip back a little IGF-1

00:09:20.992 --> 00:09:21.992
and the starvation benefit vanishes.

00:09:21.992 --> 00:09:22.992
So reducing IGF-1 signaling
may provide the dual benefits

00:09:22.992 --> 00:09:23.992
by protecting normal cells while
reducing tumor progression

00:09:23.992 --> 00:09:26.541
and may even help prevent
the cancer in the first place.

00:09:26.541 --> 00:09:27.541
As I explained before in my
"How Not To Die from Cancer" video,

00:09:27.541 --> 00:09:28.541
and an important video
about animal protein --

00:09:28.541 --> 00:09:29.541
which kind of gives away
the punch line --

00:09:29.541 --> 00:09:30.541
but fasting isn't the only way
to drop IGF-1 levels.

00:09:30.541 --> 00:09:33.920
Yes, a few days of fasting
can cut levels in half,

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:37.600
but that's largely because
you're cutting your protein intake.

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:41.579
Protein is a key determinant of
circulating IGF-1 levels in humans,

00:09:41.579 --> 00:09:43.560
suggesting that reduced
protein intake may become

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:47.110
an important component
of anticancer and anti-aging

00:09:47.110 --> 00:09:52.269
dietary interventions, particularly
a reduction in animal protein.

00:09:52.269 --> 00:09:56.110
If you compare those eating strictly
plant-based diets, getting about the

00:09:56.110 --> 00:10:00.910
recommended daily intake of protein,
0.8 grams per kg of body weight,

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:05.800
and compare them to people just
as slender but eating more typical

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:08.650
American protein intake, going
on a calorie-restricted diet

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:13.470
may lower IGF-1 a little, but eating
a plant-based diet can lower it more.

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:15.640
So not only may a diet centered
around whole plant foods

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:20.800
down-regulate IGF-1 activity,
potentially slowing the aging process,

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:26.185
but may be a way of turning
anti-aging genes against cancer.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:28.940
